.TH REPO "1" "October 2026" "repo config" "Repo Manual"
.SH NAME
repo \- repo config - manual page for repo config
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B repo
\fI\,config <name>\/\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
Summary
.PP
Read and write per\-user repo settings
.PP
repo config <name> <value>
repo config \fB\-\-unset\fR <name>
repo config \fB\-\-list\fR
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
show this help message and exit
.TP
\fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-list\fR
list all settings and their values
.TP
\fB\-\-unset\fR
remove the setting
.SS Logging options:
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
show all output
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
only show errors
.SS Multi\-manifest options:
.TP
\fB\-\-outer\-manifest\fR
operate starting at the outermost manifest
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-outer\-manifest\fR
do not operate on outer manifests
.TP
\fB\-\-this\-manifest\-only\fR
only operate on this (sub)manifest
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-this\-manifest\-only\fR, \fB\-\-all\-manifests\fR
operate on this manifest and its submanifests
.PP
Run `repo help config` to view the detailed manual.
.SH DETAILS
.PP
The 'repo config' command reads and writes repo's own per\-user settings, which
are stored in ~/.repoconfig/config (or $REPO_CONFIG_DIR/.repoconfig/config if
that environment variable is set). The file uses the git\-config format, so 'repo
config' is a convenience wrapper around:
.IP
git config \fB\-\-file\fR ~/.repoconfig/config ...
.PP
Settings are named <section>.<name>, e.g. sync.smartsync. See docs/repoconfig.md
for the list of settings repo understands.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
# Always smart sync by default.
$ repo config sync.smartsync true
.IP
# Show the current value.
$ repo config sync.smartsync
.IP
# Go back to following the manifest default.
$ repo config \-\-unset sync.smartsync
.IP
# Show all per\-user settings.
$ repo config \-\-list
